<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext policy lives in app code, not here.

    Android's network-security-config supports neither ranges nor wildcards, so
    "permit HTTP to any private-LAN address" cannot be expressed in this file.
    What used to be here instead was a hand-written list of fourteen private IPs
    — which matched a real user's LAN address only by coincidence, and which a
    user of an installed release build cannot edit. Running a local Ollama or
    MCP server over HTTP, the scenario this product is built around, therefore
    did not work for most people.

    Cleartext is permitted here so the rule can be expressed where it is
    expressible: `domain/services/CleartextPolicy.kt`. That rule is strictly
    narrower than "permit everything" — unencrypted traffic is allowed only to a
    loopback / private-LAN address the user has explicitly approved, and never
    to a public host — and it is enforced at every point that opens a connection
    to a user-configured address:

      * ModelNetworkGate.endpointRefusal()  — a server the user runs (Ollama, an
                                           OpenAI-compatible server), asked by
                                           KoogClientFactory (chat) and
                                           OllamaEmbeddingProvider (memory)
      * KoogTransportFactory               — every hop of every model client,
                                           redirects included
      * McpConnectionPool                  — every MCP connection
      * CleartextGuardInterceptor          — the shared OkHttp client, every hop
                                           (before the first lookup, and on each
                                           redirect OkHttp follows)

    The platform no longer blocks a cleartext redirect anywhere; inside the
    third-party Koog / Ktor stacks the app's own transport check does.

    GigaChat (Sber) is the one exception to "system trust anchors only". Its
    OAuth endpoint (ngw.devices.sberbank.ru) and API (api.giga.chat, and the
    legacy gigachat.devices.sberbank.ru) present certificates issued under the
    "Russian Trusted Root CA" of the Ministry of Digital Development (НУЦ
    Минцифры), which Android does not ship. That root — and its 2024 "Russian
    Trusted Sub CA", for a server that omits the intermediate — is trusted for
    those hosts ONLY, in the domain-config below: it never becomes a trust
    anchor for any other host the app reaches. The certificates in res/raw
    (PEM text; named .crt because .gitignore refuses *.pem) match the
    fingerprints Sber publishes (Root CA SHA-256 D2:6D:2D:02:31:B7:C3:9F:
    92:CC:73:85:12:BA:54:10:35:19:E4:40:5D:68:B5:BD:70:3E:97:88:CA:8E:CF:31,
    valid to 2032-02-27; Sub CA 2024 valid to 2029-07-19); replace them from
    gu-st.ru / gosuslugi.ru/crt when Минцифры reissues either.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
        `InsecureBaseConfiguration` is suppressed deliberately, not silenced: the
        header above is the reasoning, and the alternative (a hard-coded address
        list) did not work for the scenario the product exists to support. It is
        suppressed here rather than in `lint-baseline.xml` so the justification
        stays next to the setting instead of in a file nobody reads.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
    <!--
        GigaChat only. A domain-config replaces the trust anchors of base-config for
        the domains it names, so the system store is listed again; cleartext is off,
        since every GigaChat endpoint is https.
    -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <!-- ngw.devices.sberbank.ru (OAuth), gigachat.devices.sberbank.ru (legacy API) -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">devices.sberbank.ru</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">api.giga.chat</domain>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="@raw/russian_trusted_root_ca" />
            <certificates src="@raw/russian_trusted_sub_ca_2024" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
